\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Gaussian elimination in orthonormal coordinates}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Take the specified orthogonal sum and positive precision of
\ref{fdip-000D}. Choose orthonormal charts
#{C_X:X\to\mathbb R^I} and #{C_Y:Y\to\mathbb R^{J_Y}}, where #{I,J_Y}
are finite index sets and the coordinate spaces have their counting
inner products. On the disjoint-union coordinate space, the joint
chart is #{C_E=(C_X\oplus C_Y)J^{-1}}, where #{J}
is the addition isometry from that theorem.}
\p{The precision array and its components are
##{\begin{aligned}
\widehat Q&=C_EQC_E^{-1}
 =\begin{pmatrix}\widehat A&\widehat B\\
                  \widehat B^T&\widehat C\end{pmatrix},\\
\widehat A&=C_XAC_X^{-1},\qquad
\widehat B=C_XBC_Y^{-1},\\
\widehat C&=C_YCC_Y^{-1},\qquad
\widehat S=C_XSC_X^{-1}.
\end{aligned}}
Orthonormality identifies the adjoint with transpose. Thus
##{\widehat S=\widehat A-
       \widehat B\widehat C^{-1}\widehat B^T,\qquad
\det\widehat Q=\det\widehat C\det\widehat S.}
Each chart preserves [canonical Euclidean volume](fdip-002M).
Applying the intrinsic fiber formula at #{x=C_X^{-1}\xi} gives
##{\begin{aligned}
&\int_{\mathbb R^{J_Y}}
 \ue^{-\frac12(\xi,\eta)^T\widehat Q(\xi,\eta)}\,\extd\eta\\
&\qquad=c_Ce^{-\frac12\xi^T\widehat S\xi},\\
c_C&=\frac{(2\pi)^{|J_Y|/2}}{\sqrt{\det\widehat C}}.
\end{aligned}}
The determinant is unchanged by conjugation, so this is the same
raw scalar as in the intrinsic calculation. Empty index sets use
the determinant one and singleton volume one conventions.}

\p{For counting fields on #{V=R\sqcup U}, take the ordered delta
bases of #{X=\mathbb R^R} and #{Y=\mathbb R^U}. These charts are
the identity maps, #{B=H} and #{B^\dagger=H^T}.
Coordinate volume splitting in \ref{fdip-002J} recovers the usual
#{\extd x\,\extd y} formula. The carrier, split, precision and volumes are
supplied; the resulting pair #{(S,c_C)} is relative to those data.}

\p{\citet{Appendix A.5.5, pp. 650-651}{boyd2004convex} uses its
first block as pivot. Ordering the coordinates as #{(\eta,\xi)}
puts #{\widehat C} first and gives the eliminated pivot above.
Its square-completion formula applies because #{\widehat Q}
is real symmetric positive definite and #{\widehat C} is invertible.}
\p{The density in
\citet{Appendix A.2, equation (A.4), p. 200}{rasmussen2006gaussian}
is parametrized by covariance. Substituting #{\widehat C^{-1}}
for that covariance gives
##{(2\pi)^{-|J_Y|/2}\sqrt{\det\widehat C}\,
 \ue^{-\frac12\eta^T\widehat C\eta}.}
Its reciprocal prefactor is #{c_C}. The intrinsic proof also
specifies the volume and proves the determinant identity.}

\p{For finite decidable index types, Mathlib's
\leanref{Matrix.schur_complement_eq₂₂} supplies the corresponding
quadratic identity over a commutative star ring when the eliminated
block is Hermitian and invertible. Apply it over #{\mathbb R}
with its blocks #{A,B,D} equal to
#{\widehat A,\widehat B,\widehat C}; its lower-left block is
#{\widehat B^T}. Mathlib's \leanref{Matrix.det_fromBlocks₂₂}
has finite decidable indices and a commutative coefficient ring,
with invertible eliminated block #{D}. In its four-block notation,
#{(A,B,C,D)} here means
#{(\widehat A,\widehat B,\widehat B^T,\widehat C)}.}
\p{TauCeti's \leanref/tauceti{Matrix.PosDef.gaussianCondCov}
gives strict positivity of the Schur complement of a real
positive-definite block matrix, with finite retained and eliminated
indices. Apply its joint matrix to #{\widehat Q}.
Despite its conditional-covariance name, this is an algebraic
application to precision. These declarations supply neither the
fiber integral nor its reference-volume convention.}
